      "problem": "At which points on the curve $9{x}^{2}y=3{y}^{3}-6x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}y & =3{y}^{3}-6x \\\\ 9{x}^{2}y-3{y}^{3}+6x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y-3{y}^{3}+6x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(9{x}^{2}y-3{y}^{3}+6x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(9{x}^{2}y-3{y}^{3}+6x)j \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})i-(18xy+6)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((9{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,i-(18xy+6)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}y & =3{y}^{3}{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 9{x}^{2}(-x) & =3(-x)^{3}-6x \\\\ -9{x}^{3} & =-3{x}^{3}-6x \\\\ -6{x}^{3}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(1-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}y & =3{y}^{3}{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 9{x}^{2}(x) & =3(x)^{3}-6x \\\\ 9{x}^{3} & =3{x}^{3}-6x \\\\ 6{x}^{3}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(1+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get that $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(1,-1).$",
